<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Dahomey</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Dahomey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of Dahomey was a West African kingdom centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abomey">Abomey</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, known for its centralised monarchy and its role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. In the eighteenth century it was forced to pay tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> after repeated Oyo cavalry invasions. It later freed itself from Oyo's overlordship and was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the 1890s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Dahomey" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samhanin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_banner_of_B%C3%A9hanzin_of_Dahomey.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…1890s French columns pushed east to the Niger, defeating the Tukulor state of Ahmadu, taking Timbuktu in 1894 and fighting a long war against Samori Ture, who was captured in 1898, while other expeditions conquered the <b>Kingdom of Dahomey</b> in 1892–1894 and occupied the Mossi kingdoms in 1896–1897.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Repeated invasions of <b>Dahomey</b> from 1726 onwards, after King Agaja’s conquests of Allada and Ouidah had upset the coastal trading order, forced Dahomey to pay tribute under Alaafin Ojigi, and a treaty of 1748 confirmed it as a tribute-paying vassal.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Vodou, blending Catholic ritual with the religions of Guinea, Congo and <b>Dahomey</b>, was outlawed and driven underground, but healed and held them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The word Vodou comes from the Fon language of West Africa: Vôdoun, a word for a spirit or deity in the kingdom of <b>Dahomey</b>, was first recorded in the Doctrina Christiana of 1658.</blockquote><a href="/religion/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The neighbouring Fon of <b>Dahomey</b> adopted Ifa divination as Fa, and Yoruba gods entered the religion of their vodun.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A king who broke his oaths could be destooled, as Kusi Obodom was after a failed invasion of <b>Dahomey</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ashanti">Social Hierarchy of the Ashanti Empire</a></li></ul></section>
